===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|anti-matter}}

===Etymology===
From {{prefix|en|anti|matter}}. {{coin|en|Arthur Schuster|nat=British|occ=physicist|nobycat=1|in=1898}} to describe matter that resists gravity in a jocular article in ''{{w|Nature (journal)|Nature}}'' titled "Potential Matter.—A Holiday Dream", but not used in a modern sense until the 1940s.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæn.tiˌmæt.ɚ/|/ˈænˌtaɪˌmæt.ɚ/|[ˈæn.tiˌmæ.ɾɚ]|[ˈæ.ɾ̃iˌmæ.ɾɚ]|[ˈæ.niˌmæ.ɾɚ]|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-antimatter.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|physics}} [[matter|Matter]] that is composed of the [[antiparticle]]s of those that [[constitute]] normal matter.
#* {{quote-journal|en-GB|author=Hannah Devlin|title=Scientists find antimatter is subject to gravity|work=The Guardian|date=2023-09-27|url=https://www.theguardian.com/science/2023/sep/27/scientists-find-antimatter-is-subject-to-gravity|issn=0261-3077|passage=But most theories predict that equal amounts of matter and '''antimatter''' should have been produced during the big bang, and the mystery of what happened to all the '''antimatter''' is a central question in fundamental physics.}}
# {{lb|en|physics}} A form of matter that has a key property, such as [[charge]], opposite to that of ordinary matter.
#* {{quote-journal|en-GB|author=Alok Jha|title=We can't see antimatter but it really does matter|work=The Guardian|date=2010-11-19|url=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2010/nov/19/antimatter-cern-antihydrogen|issn=0261-3077|passage=Particles of matter and '''antimatter''' are identical, except for an opposite electrical charge. An electron has a negative charge whereas its antiparticle, the positron, has a positive charge, and both have an identical mass.}}

